The second parenting strategy is Handling constrained alignments, which builds upon the previous section and provides an analysis for w-anchored DTW distance with a different approximation factor of c=4d3/2(m1+m2). The proof follows from Lemma 6 by mimicking the one for the w-anchored Fr\u00e9chet distance in Lemma 12.

The third section, Handling constrained alignments, introduces a hashing scheme that guarantees certain probabilities and approximation factors based on the traversal width (w), an arbitrary integer, \u03b4, and curve lengths m1 and m2. The theorem states that if dw,aDTW(P, Q) < r, then P hash to the same bucket with a probability greater than 1/sqrt(2*w/(2m/l)), where l is an arbitrary integer. On the other hand, if d(w+2(\u03b4-1)),aDTW (P, Q) > cr, then there exists no collision between /hatwidePi and /hatwideQi for each i, resulting in a probability of 0.

The percentages are calculated based on the importance and relevance of the information provided by each child to the overall understanding of the parenting strategy being discussed. In this case, both children provide important details about the hashing scheme's guarantees when dealing with DTW distance and constrained alignments. Therefore, I would assign a higher percentage to these sections as they are crucial for understanding how the hashing scheme works in different scenarios (i.e., when dw,aDTW(P, Q) < r or d(w+2(\u03b4-1)),aDTW (P, Q)> cr).

